          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p><strong>Leah:</strong> So, what is a database?</p>
<p>A database is an ‘organised structured collection of data’ which can be easily managed. The data is normally organised in tables which contain rows, columns, and indexes that make it easier to search and find relevant information. There are many different types of databases but before we go into these first let’s first start by looking at ‘unstructured data’.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> Unstructured data is not organised and can be stored in a number of ways.</p>
<p>For example, a letter has ‘structure’ in the way it is arranged on the page [e.g. Date, address, salutation, heading, letter content, signature etc], but the actual ‘data’ in the letter would be ‘unstructured’ so would not be able to be manipulated in any way.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> However, if that same letter was used as a template for a mail-merge, then the data that would be used to generate the output letters, would in fact be ‘structured data’ as the data would be stored in a structured way as in a table such names and addresses of recipients.</p>
<p>Here are some items that would be classified as unstructured data:</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> • Emails – these could be business or personal</p>
<p>• Document files such as letters, reports, presentations</p>
<p>• Websites – most web content is regarded as unprocessed and unstructured, and on some websites the unstructured data could be used for SEO [Search Engine Optimisation].</p>
<p>• Media such as images, video, or audio</p>
<p>• Mobile data such as text messages</p>
<p>• Communication such as team chats</p>
<p>• Social media – although technically a website this is a little different as most social media websites are considered both unstructured and structured data. There are some elements which is unstructured data such as a user post, but there are other elements which would be stored as structured data such as user details.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> It is important to recognise the distinction between ‘structured’ and ‘structured data’ as many of the these ‘items’ we have mentioned are in fact ‘structured’ like a letter, but their data is ‘unstructured’.</p>
<p>Nearly all types of Databases contain data that is ‘structured’. By structured we mean data that can be organised, analysed, indexed, searched, and easy to understand.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> Here is a table of data that is ‘unstructured’</p>
<p>Now if we were to ‘structure’ this data it would look like this and be much easier to understand.</p>
<p>There are many different types of databases used today and most are enclosed within a database management system [DBMS] within which the data is stored in tables and can be manipulated or analysed using queries.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> A database uses structured query language [SQL] to do this. SQL is often used by businesses to run statistical reports, so, for example a college could generate a report from their ‘learning database’ using a query to ask, ‘how many learners are studying a specific course in a specific month’.</p>
<p>Databases make it easy to store information that is quick and easy to search for, due to their index capabilities, and most websites use databases to store data. A lot of websites use MySQL which is an ‘open-source’ Relational database system.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> Nonrelational databases [NoSQL] have become more popular in recent years as they offer a more flexible, scalable alternative to the traditional SQL ‘Relational database management system’ [RDBMS]. RDBMS work better for data that is more simple and static. NoSQL databases allow the use of ‘unstructured data’ whereas RDBMS are table based and therefore structured. So, for example, in the case of websites and social media, the unstructured data could be used for SEO or marketing campaigns.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> Cloud databases are stored on the ‘Cloud’ which could be private, public or a hybrid cloud platform and there are two types – ‘traditional’ or ‘Database as a service’ [DBaaS].</p>
<p>Open source databases can be SQL or NoSQL and as we have said one of the most well used is ‘MySQL’ which is an ‘RDBMS’.</p>
<p>Other common database types include ‘Object-oriented’, ‘Distributed’, ‘Graph’, ‘OLTP databases’ and ‘Data warehouse’. Less common types which are more specific to their areas of work and specialism include ‘Multi-model’, ‘Document/JSON’, and ‘Self-driving databases’.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> Check out our website section on this page to find out more.</p>

